   133	- the census, merge and reconciliation briefs;
   134	- the mapping brief (A2's);
   135	- the card template (Deck 1's);
   136	- the council-marking protocol (Deck 1's `PROTOCOL.md` and `council_mark.py`, unchanged);
   137	- the control specifications;
   138	- the seeds;
   139	- `score_comparison.py`, with tests that each fail on a mutant of the rule they hold.
   140	
   141	### 1.6 The new system as run: three more code changes, each with a test and a mutant
   142	
   143	- **C: `run_fresh.py --limits PATH`** for Step 1. The frozen `stage2-limits.json` stays the default and stays in the unseeded freeze. The
   144	  comparison's file sets max_calls 6,000 (1,976 articles × 3 attempts) and max_seconds 172,800 (48 h). It is hashed. With the shared ledger,
   145	  Step 1's own dollar cap does not apply (`live.py` line 84).
   146	- **D: astra.**
   147	  - `stage_a_astra.py` sets `stage_a.SETTING` to gpt-6-astra (the pattern of `gate1_candidate.py` lines 26 and 101) and refuses, **before
   148	    transport**, any request that differs from the frozen request except in the model string (that file's lines 65 and 66).
   149	  - `run_fresh.py --ladder-variant astra-frozen` calls it, and checks every pass's archive against the astra configuration checksum written
   150	    at stage 0.
   151	  - `stage_a.py` is not edited.
   152	  - The three passes stay. The judge reads pass 1. Passes 2 and 3 feed the stability checks in §1.7.
   153	- **E: the judge to the cutoff.** `run_stage_b.py --end-day`, threaded from `run_fresh._judge`, set to 09-20. It is proved on a stored run
   154	  extended by a trailing day with no articles: due questions close, and nothing is sent.
   155	
   156	### 1.7 The checks D665 keeps beside the verdict, each with its tool
   157	
   158	| Check | Status | Tool and input |
   159	|---|---|---|
   160	| Figure ranges meet end to end; the favourable end is the same in every pass; the due day is the same in ≥ 90% of passes | **Hard** (D665): a failure is reported with the verdict as a release blocker | Code over every new-system draw's three passes |
   161	| D400's reader lines (1–5) | Reported | The October Stage A reading (`stage_a/build_reader_packets.py`, the two-family tally) on every draw's pass 1, after the verdict reading |
   162	| Step 2's three lines (D427) | Kept, as D427 set them | The Step 2 log §12 method (`step2-validation-2026-11-07/`: retrieval, merge and dossier scripts) on the new system's three draws |
   163	| D431's frozen-reference reading, 07-01 to 07-14 | Printed beside | Stage B's reading on each new-system draw's judgements at 07-14, against the frozen judgement gold |
   164	| The proposed absolute floors (reflection-10) | Printed beside | Three come from this comparison's own cards: coverage (≥ 80%), trivial or duplicate (≤ 20%), supported (≥ 90%). The citation and omission floors need source-first readings this plan does not run; they are printed as **not measured** |
   165	
   166	### 1.7a Preflight
   167	
   168	- Every stored answer of the latest runs of both systems goes through the current checks (AGENTS §5).
   169	- Both launchers run dry with fake answers over two days, with every code change in place.
   170	
   171	## 2. Stage 1: the slice (wiring and rates)
   172	
   173	- **What runs:** one draw of each system, 07-01 to 07-07 (about 98 articles), with all code changes, on its own ledger.
   174	- **Pass checks:**
   175	  - both draws complete;
   176	  - Step 1 averages ≤ 51 s an article (1.5 × the measured 34 s);
   177	  - Step 1 costs ≤ $0.0153 an article (1.5 × $0.0102);
   178	  - every answer-choice request was checked by the wrapper before transport, and every archive shows astra;
   179	  - production's day lines are complete.
   180	- **On a failure:** stop, log it, and fix only the launcher. Anything else returns to the council, and then to Alex.
   181	
   182	## 3. Stage 2: the full run
   183	
   184	- **What runs:** three draws of each system, 07-01 to 09-20, all six at once on one shared ledger. They are launched detached, each with a log
   185	  and a pid (AGENTS §9).
   186	- **D449's rules unchanged:**
   187	  - a format or transport failure stops that draw's component; one redo follows in a new folder;
   188	  - for Step 1, the redo starts from empty, about 17 hours more;
   189	  - a second stop, a crash, a dead launcher, a wrong configuration or a ledger refusal stops the run.
   190	- **The risk, measured:**
   191	  - the 4 stored Step 1 failures (of 2,549 articles) were all dropped connections from before the network retry (2026-11-15);
   192	  - after it, 0 in 624;
   193	  - 9 format retries, all recovered.
   194	- **A stopped ledger refuses every later launch.** A relaunch is a new ledger and six fresh draws, decided by the council.
   195	
   196	## 4. Stage 3 (after the run): mapping, cards, marking, verdict
   197	
   198	1. **Read the libraries at the cutoff,** by snapshot (never a live database).
   199	2. **Mapping** (§1.4) by both families, apart, with third readers on disagreements.
   200	3. **Cards**, with system labels hidden and order shuffled (key outside the repository):
   201	   - every covering question;
   202	   - a guardrail sample of 35 questions per system-draw, drawn from its library by seed 20261117;
   203	   - the controls: 6 planted wrong answers (a rung moved, 3 per system), 4 supported controls confirmed first by a separate reader, and 3
   204	     hidden repeats. Controls are outside every product count.
   205	4. **The council marks every card** by Deck 1's protocol. Only void sessions are rerun; there is no reread of a missed plant. Then
   206	   `score_comparison.py` applies `verdict-rule.json`.
   207	5. **The §1.7 checks.**
   208	6. **The report to Alex** (plain words):
   209	   - the verdict first;
   210	   - a, b and N, under both countings;
   211	   - the company-figures subgroup;
   212	   - every split card for his spot check;
   213	   - the §1.7 table;
   214	   - `FINDINGS.md`;
   215	   - the cost and the time.
   216	
   217	   No release engineering resumes until he answers.
   218	
   219	## 5. Estimates (measured rates; checked at the slice)
   220	
   221	**New system, per draw:**
   222	- Step 1: 1,976 articles at about 30 to 34 s (measured: 26.1 s on fresh-a2, about 31 s on e2e-full c1, 33.7 s on the carried week) is
   223	  about 16.5 to 19 h. At $0.0089 to $0.0102 an article, that is $18 to $20.
   224	- Step 2: about $3.
   225	- The answer-choice step: about 55 theses × 3 passes × $0.037 to $0.050, about $6 to $8.
   226	- The judge over 82 days: about $5 to $10 (an estimate, the least certain).
   227	- **About $32 to $41 and about 20 h a draw.**
   228	
   229	**Old system, per draw:** poc-v118's own $0.53 to $0.66 for 243 articles, scaled to 1,976, is about $5. About 2 to 3 h.
   230	
   231	**The whole run:**
   232	- about $110 to $140, and about 20 h of wall clock (the six draws at once);
   233	- the $250 shared-ledger guard includes concurrent reservations (a Step 1 reservation is about $0.12).
   234	
   235	**The census and reading:**
   236	- 18 census sessions, 2 merges, 2 reconciliations and the third readers: a few hours;
   237	- the cards, about (N + 35) × 6 + 13: roughly 330 to 450 cards. Marked by the council, about 6 to 10 h.
   238	
   239	## 6. Monitoring (sources, thresholds, actions)
   240	
   241	- **Sources:**
   242	  - the shared `ledger.json` and `ledger.attempts.jsonl` (attempts and spend per pid, hence per draw and component);
   243	  - each draw's `run.json` component states;
   244	  - production's `replay.log` day lines;
   245	  - the launcher logs.
   246	
   247	  Never a live database (AGENTS §9).
   248	- **Monitor:** one `Monitor` emits a line on:
   249	  - every component state change, stop, crash, refusal or redo;
   250	  - every 100 Step 1 attempts per clean draw;
   251	  - every 7 days of each production draw.
   252	- **Heartbeat:** every 20 minutes, spend per completed article per draw, against the slice's measured rate. Like is compared with like:
   253	  clean draws with each other, and production draws with each other.
   254	- **Thresholds and actions:**
   255	  - **Clean draw stalled:** no ledger attempt for 35 minutes (a call may wait 3 × 600 s plus 90 s before failing).
   256	  - **Production draw stalled:** no new day line for 45 minutes.
   257	  - On a stall, I read the logs and the process state within one heartbeat.
   258	  - **The only stops I make:**
   259	    - a runaway: spend per article above 2 times the slice's rate over at least 50 articles;
   260	    - a hang: the process at 0% CPU beyond the stall limit.
   261	
   262	    Either stops the run through the ledger's own stop, with the reason.
   263	  - Everything else (a redo, a slow draw) is logged and watched.
   264	- **`RUN-LOG.md`:** one dated row per event, check and heartbeat that changes anything, with numbers. It is reported to Alex as results land.
   265	
   266	## 7. Returns to Alex
   267	
   268	- the verdict, whichever it is;
   269	- N < 20 at the census;
   270	- a stop of the run that is not a launcher wiring fault;
   271	- the $250 guard reached;
   272	- a hard check failing;
   273	- no verdict by 2026-12-01.

